Q: Changing HtmlOutput in Google Apps Script Web App A: <p>Here is an example of using GAS to replace the contents of the HTML body.  Although I am using a spreadsheet as my basis, I believe it should work with a Web App.  I add a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> to the body so as not to disturb the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> section.</p>
<p><strong>Code.gs</strong></p>
<pre><code>function include(filename) {
  return HtmlService.createHtmlOutputFromFile(filename).getContent();
}

function showTest() {
  let html = HtmlService.createTemplateFromFile(&quot;HTML_Body&quot;);
  SpreadsheetApp.getUi().showModalDialog(html.evaluate(),&quot;Test&quot;);
}

function getHtml(id) {
  let fileName = id === &quot;buttonA&quot; ? &quot;HTML_B&quot; : &quot;HTML_A&quot;;
  let html = HtmlService.createHtmlOutputFromFile(fileName);
  return html.getContent();
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>HTML_Body.html</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;base target=&quot;_top&quot;&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id=&quot;htmlBody&quot;&gt;
      &lt;?!= include(&quot;HTML_A&quot;); ?&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;?!= include(&quot;JS_ButtonOnClick&quot;); ?&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>HTML_A.html</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;This is A&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;input id=&quot;buttonA&quot; type=&quot;button&quot; value=&quot;Click Me&quot; onclick=&quot;buttonOnClick(this)&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>HTML_B.html</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;This is B&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;input id=&quot;buttonB&quot; type=&quot;button&quot; value=&quot;Click Me&quot; onclick=&quot;buttonOnClick(this)&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>JS_ButtonOnClick</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
  function buttonOnClick(button) {
    try {
      google.script.run.withSuccessHandler( 
        function(html) {
          document.getElementById(&quot;htmlBody&quot;).innerHTML = html;
        }
      ).getHtml(button.id);
    }
    catch(err) {
      alert(err);
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>References</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/html/best-practices" rel="nofollow noreferrer">HTML Best Practices</a></li>
<li><a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/html/reference/run" rel="nofollow noreferrer">google.script.run()</a></li>
</ul>

Q: Is it possible to loop over a test implementing a different fixture per loop? A: <p>I think what you are looking for is to parameterize your test using an option and then creating a describe block for that option, e.g. like described <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-test#test-describe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> under &quot;Anonymized group&quot;.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>test.describe('some/endpoint', () =&gt; {
    for (const user of users) {
      test.describe(`user=${user.name}`, () =&gt; {
        test.use({ user });
        test('GET', async ({ user, context }) =&gt; {
          await context.request.get('/some/endpoint');
        })
      });
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This requires some infrastructure work first though:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Define users as an array of user objects as desired.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Add an option fixture 'user' that expects a user object or null</p>
</li>
<li><p>Override the context fixture in your test suite to depend on the user option.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If user is set during context execution, create the user (if required) and login as the user to create the storageState for that context (create a temporary page from the passed in context if you need). Cleanup the user after the test run again (i.e. put the cleanup after the <code>await use(context)</code> call).</p>
</li>
<li><p>The tests will now run logged in as the user specified in test.use(...).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>You can improve the fixture flow by:</p>
<ul>
<li>Add an additional <code>materializedUser</code> fixture that depends on <code>user</code> and creates the user in the database if they do not exist and cleanup the user after the run if they were created. Have the <code>context</code> fixture depend on the <code>materializedUser</code> fixture instead. That will give you more separation of concerns.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>  materializedUser: async ({user}, use) =&gt; {
    if (!user) {
      return await use(undefined);
    }

    const existingUser = await loadUser(user);
    if (existingUser) {
      return await use(existingUser);
    }

    try {
      await use(await createUser(user));
    } finally {
        await deleteUser(user);
    }
  }
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Overriding the <code>storageState</code> fixture instead of the <code>context</code> fixture. This will also require you to create an ad-hoc context if you can't easily create a storageState object without the help of a browser and page.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>  storageState: async ({materializedUser, browser}, use) =&gt; {
    if (!materializedUser) {
      return await use(undefined);
    }

    await use(await getUserStorageState(browser, materializedUser));
  }
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>If you are heavily depending on pre-created users instead of creating new test users per test run, you'll want to cache and reuse the <code>storageState</code> object instead of logging in over again for each and every test. In my experience this creates more headaches than is worth. Creating fresh test users for every test run is the way to go. Paramterize the user object to your liking (e.g. <code>test.use({ user: getTestUser({ role: 'administator' }) })</code> ) if you need a &quot;special&quot; user.</li>
</ul>

Q: What is the idiomatic Go equivalent of C&#39;s ternary operator? A: <p>While this is quite an old question, for anyone reading now, you can make your own ternary function:</p>
<pre><code>func Ternary(condition bool, a interface{}, b interface{}) interface{} {
    if condition {
        return a
    }
    return b
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or if your using go 1.18+, you can also use <code>github.com/samber/lo</code> which has <code>lo.Ternary</code> as well as several other utility functions which I believe many will find quite useful.</p>
